#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}\n$$\n\nwhich has order $3$.\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166584 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n2. $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order equation is autonomous if it can be written as $y'=f(y)$ with no explicit dependence on $x$.\n\n- I is $y'=y^3$, so it is autonomous.\n- II rearranges to $y'=(y^3+2)/x$ for $x\\ne0$. Its right-hand side explicitly depends on $x$, so it is not autonomous.\n- III rearranges to $y'=1+3\\sqrt y$, which depends only on $y$, so it is autonomous.\n\nThe correct answer is **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n2. $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n3. $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6$.\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e$.\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2$. This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a362f3bc-f3de-5a24-8bdc-2e5de1afd3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165811} {:answer/id #uuid "b82ea3d8-9cf2-5b0c-9f5c-edae9c45197c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165812} {:answer/id #uuid "bed30f47-0163-5d27-b917-d60e8135d88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165813} {:answer/id #uuid "ffdde2f1-d7a5-5e8e-b1fe-2c3df6a68b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165814} {:answer/id #uuid "3932c714-868e-5af6-8f57-faa5aef0076b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed30f47-0163-5d27-b917-d60e8135d88e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165813} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7830fd-e7fa-50b5-a61e-222206b0390d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6d57756-51a0-53da-bab4-6debeaf1e4ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-95982" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + x^{3}y = 5y^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The highest derivative appearing is $d^2y/dx^2$. The powers $x^3$ and $y^4$ do not determine the order. Therefore the ODE has order **2**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce4acdd-0589-54c7-822a-272cb00980e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165829} {:answer/id #uuid "56387554-f843-5d1d-b7bb-91da91584863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165830} {:answer/id #uuid "6740aa00-f0bc-57d2-a30a-68e33996886b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165831} {:answer/id #uuid "6e83c890-0b59-5c1a-8da0-0162d2823067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165832} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1fc708-37b8-5fbb-9d3a-5052130cb2be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ce4acdd-0589-54c7-822a-272cb00980e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165829} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a4ceaf6-15ec-5b6b-8a59-7a6136b14b74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c01fd7ca-ed83-5e17-8036-cd03a49c52f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95985" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $2xy^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 1$\n2. $y^{\\prime } - 2xy = e^{y}$\n3. $2xy^{\\prime \\prime } = y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 1, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "System I combines a first-order equation with a specified value $y(0)=1$, so it has the form of a first-order IVP. II has no initial condition. III contains $y''$ and is second order. Select **I only**.\n\nThe classification does not assert existence: system I has no differentiable solution satisfying the equation at $x=0$, because substituting its initial data gives $0=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8fcc14-3b71-5da6-b5dd-7babd909bb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166171} {:answer/id #uuid "cc48ca6b-4aa3-5db8-bea8-d0c431202662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} {:answer/id #uuid "38f746bf-71f6-5862-b734-46ca998885c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166173} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa5b041-244e-5910-a46e-1d2d8b63ba6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166174} {:answer/id #uuid "61ba28ba-32e1-59ce-87b7-bf847008593c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc48ca6b-4aa3-5db8-bea8-d0c431202662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166172} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a53d5f-04a9-5740-b2e4-3c2ac8d9a527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed0b0a68-7ad2-55d7-8d80-82f9a5c1ced6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96986" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n1. $\\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y^{\\prime \\prime } = 3y^{\\prime }, y(1) = 2, y^{\\prime }(1) = 1$\n2. $2x^{2}y^{\\prime } = y, y(0) = 2$\n3. $4y^{\\prime } - \\frac{1}{3}xy = \\ln y$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "System I is second order because it contains $y''$. System II is a first-order equation with one initial condition. System III has no initial condition. Thus **II only** has the form of a first-order IVP.\n\nSystem II is inconsistent at its initial point: $2(0)^2y'(0)=y(0)$ would require $0=2$. An IVP may be posed without having a solution."}]}
